name: awk
boss: Indexer
flavor: Zero-indexed. Off by one and you get nothing.
brief: awk splits every line into fields and runs your program on each one.
brief: $1 is the first field, $NF the last, $0 the whole line. NR is the line
brief: number. That is most of it.
brief:
brief: A program is pattern { action }. Leave off the pattern and it runs on
brief: every line; leave off the action and it prints the line. BEGIN runs
brief: before the first line, END after the last -- which is where totals go.
brief:
brief: Where cut takes columns, awk takes columns AND does arithmetic, keeps
brief: counters, and holds a map. That is why it outlives cut in your fingers.
---
setup: @log@ lines run "date time SEV message"
q: %show% just the severity field of each @log@ line
a: awk '{print $3}' @log@
out: ^INFO$
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: $3 is the third field; fields split on whitespace by default
---
q: @log@ lines may end in an ip -- %show% the last field of each
a: awk '{print $NF}' @log@
cmp: ref
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: NF is the number of fields, so $NF is the last one
---
q: %show% just the date and severity from @log@, side by side
a: awk '{print $1, $3}' @log@
out: 2024-03-01 INFO
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: a comma between print arguments inserts a space in the output
---
q: %show% every line of @log@ with its line number in front
a: awk '{print NR, $0}' @log@
out: ^1 2024
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: $0 is the whole line, unsplit
---
q: %count% the lines in @log@ using awk
a: awk 'END {print NR}' @log@
out: ^[0-9]+$
cmp: ref
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: END runs once, after the last line, and NR is still set
---
q: %show% only the lines of @log@ that mention @sev@
a: awk '/@sev@/' @log@
out: @sev@
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: a pattern with no action means "print the line"
---
q: %show% just the time of each @sev@ line in @log@
a: awk '/@sev@/ {print $2}' @log@
cmp: ref
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: the pattern filters which lines run the action
---
setup: @csv@ is name,id,status,count
q: %show% just the name column of @csv@
a: awk -F, '{print $1}' @csv@
out: ^gateway$
ref: awk
teach: awk -F
hint: -F sets the field separator, same idea as cut -d
---
q: %show% the name column of @csv@, skipping the header row
a: awk -F, 'NR > 1 {print $1}' @csv@
out: ^gateway$
ref: awk
teach: awk -F
hint: NR is the line number, so NR>1 means "not the first line"
---
q: {add up the count column of @csv@, skipping the header row|sum the count column of @csv@, ignoring the header|total the count column of @csv@ without the header}
a: awk -F, 'NR > 1 {s += $4} END {print s}' @csv@
out: ^[0-9]+$
cmp: ref
ref: awk
teach: awk -F
hint: s starts at zero (uninitialized) and accumulates with +=
---
q: %show% the largest value in the count column of @csv@
a: awk -F, 'NR > 1 && $4 > m {m = $4} END {print m}' @csv@
cmp: ref
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: m starts at zero (uninitialized) and gets replaced whenever a bigger value shows up
---
q: %count% how many times each severity level appears in @log@
a: awk '{c[$3]++} END {for (k in c) print k, c[k]}' @log@
cmp: ref
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: awk arrays are maps -- c[$3]++ counts per distinct value
---
q: @log@ mixes severities -- %show% the first of each
a: awk '!seen[$3]++' @log@
out: INFO
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: the counter is zero the first time, which is false, which prints
---
q: %show% rows of @csv@ where the status field is up
a: awk -F, '$3 == "up"' @csv@
out: gateway
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: a bare condition with no action still means print
---
q: %show% rows of @csv@ whose count field is above 10
a: awk -F, 'NR > 1 && $4 > 10' @csv@
cmp: ref
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: && combines two conditions -- both must be true
---
q: %show% the name column of @csv@, header "host" printed first
a: awk -F, 'BEGIN {print "host"} {print $1}' @csv@
out: ^host$
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: BEGIN runs before the first line is read
---
q: {pass the literal string @word@ into awk as a variable called w and print it|hand awk the literal @word@ as a variable named w, then print it|give awk a variable w set to the literal string @word@ and print it back}
a: awk -v w=@word@ 'BEGIN {print w}'
out: @word@
ref: awk
teach: awk -v
hint: -v sets an awk variable before the program runs
---
q: %show% name and status from @csv@ joined by a colon, not a space
a: awk -F, -v OFS=: '{print $1, $3}' @csv@
out: gateway:up
ref: awk
teach: awk -v
hint: OFS is the output field separator
---
q: %show% how many comma-separated fields each line of @csv@ has
a: awk -F, '{print NF}' @csv@
cmp: ref
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: NF is the field count for the current line
---
q: {print @log@'s first field, giving awk its program via -e|pass awk its program with -e to print @log@'s first field|use -e, not a positional program, to print @log@'s first field}
a: awk -e '{print $1}' @log@
out: 2024-03-01
ref: awk
teach: awk -e
hint: -e names the program explicitly, the role the positional argument usually plays
---
q: @log@ may mention @sev@ -- match only the actual severity field
a: awk '$3 ~ /@sev@/' @log@
out: @sev@
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: ~ tests a field against a regex; !~ is its negation

---
q: %list% severities in @log@ by frequency, most frequent first
a: awk '{print $3}' @log@ | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
cmp: ref
ref: uniq
teach: uniq -c
hint: extract the column, sort it, count it, then sort the counts numerically
